db -- Command

Assembler-level symbolic debugger
db [-cdefort] [object file] [auxiliary file]

db works on the level of assembly language, on the symbol table the compiler
and the assembler build into an l.out object.  It runs a program as a child
under trace control, reads a core file, reads a file as a flat array of
bytes, or reads the running kernel, and in each case prints and patches what
it finds.  Using it needs a working knowledge of the Z8001.

What the two file arguments mean depends on the option that chose the mode,
and each mode has its own defaults:

     no option   With no arguments, the object file is l.out and the
                 auxiliary file is core.  One argument names the object
                 file; two name the object file and the core file.
     -c          The auxiliary file is a core file, l.out and core by
                 default.  One argument names the core file; two name the
                 object file and the core file.
     -d          A system dump: /coherent and /dev/dump by default,
                 otherwise both named.
     -e          The argument is an object file, which db executes as a
                 child process under trace control.
     -f          Map the file as a straight array of bytes.  One argument
                 names that file; two name the object file and the file.
     -k          Map a kernel: /coherent and /dev/mem by default.  One
                 argument names the memory file; two name both.
     -o          The argument is an object file, l.out by default; with two
                 arguments the second is a second object file.

Only one of -c, -d, -e, -f, -k and -o may be given.  The rest of the
options modify whichever mode was chosen:

     -h          Map a segmented object at virtual base 0x300000 instead of
                 the usual one.  The source calls this debugging coherent.
     -r          Open the files read-only, even where they could be written.
     -s          Do not read the symbol table.
     -t          Do input and output through /dev/tty, so that the program
                 being debugged may have the terminal.

The usage message db prints for a bad option is

     Usage: db [-cdefort] [object file] [auxiliary file]

which lists neither -h, -k nor -s, though all three are accepted.

Files
l.out      the default object file
core       the default core file
/coherent  the default kernel image, for -d and -k
/dev/dump  the default system dump, for -d
/dev/mem   the default kernel memory, for -k

